From cf18ac82f945ee144d1efb5ad48a999fdda3066a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 18:36:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] serial: qcom-geni: release the OPP and ICC votes in system
 sleep instead of force-suspending

Since commit d0cd9c8d0fd5 ("serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume
to system sleep callbacks") an open geni UART is power-collapsed for
system sleep: pm_runtime_force_suspend() turns the serial engine's
clocks and interconnect vote off in the normal suspend phase and they
stay off until pm_runtime_force_resume() in the normal resume phase.

On the Surface Pro 11 (X1E80100) the Surface Aggregator EC hangs off
such a UART (ttyHS0, b88000, 4 MHz, hardware flow control). On 7.3 a
suspend that closely follows a resume can leave the EC link dead: the
D0-entry notification and every later SAM request time out with
-ETIMEDOUT, the battery and AC resume callbacks block in sequential
timeouts, and the machine looks hard-locked (it panics in
dpm_watchdog_handler() with CONFIG_DPM_WATCHDOG). The first 7.3 builds
hung within one to three "close lid, open lid, suspend again within
30 s" cycles; later builds without this patch hung far less often (once
in about sixteen suspends on one, never in forty on two others with
extra logging), so it is a timing window, not a fixed sequence. 7.2
never showed it.

Simply dropping the forced suspend avoids the hang but keeps the CX
rail from collapsing, because the baud-rate OPP vote then stays in the
RPMh sleep set (0 % CX residency measured over a 2 h sleep).

Release only the votes (OPP rate 0, interconnect bandwidth off) in the
system suspend callback and restore them on resume, keeping the engine
clocked and its pins in their default state. Like
pm_runtime_force_suspend()/pm_runtime_force_resume(), disable runtime PM
across the transition and leave a closed, runtime-suspended port alone:
its votes are already released, and restoring them on resume would leave
it holding an interconnect vote while it stays runtime suspended.
Disabling runtime PM before the check also keeps a dedicated wake IRQ
from runtime-resuming a closed port before the wake IRQs are armed,
which would leave the port holding its votes for the whole sleep.

Why the clocked engine avoids the hang is not established. The port
uses hardware flow control, so the serial core does not drive RFR
itself, and RX is cancelled on both paths. Setting UPSTAT_AUTOCTS, so
that the core cannot latch a deasserted CTS at resume, did not prevent
the hang. What this patch restores is that the engine stays clocked
through the transition, as on 7.2, where device_prepare() holds the
runtime PM reference that would otherwise let it gate.

Measured on the Surface Pro 11: no link loss in 9/9 trigger cycles and
none since in daily use, CX/AOSS/DDR residency 99.5 % over a 1.8 h
sleep at ~0.2 W, the same as the forced path and as 7.2. On a Lenovo
Yoga Slim 7x (no EC on a geni UART): Bluetooth works after resume, ten
suspends each 10 s after the previous resume all resumed, and CX, AOSS
and DDR were collapsed for 1192 s of a 1205 s sleep.

Fixes: d0cd9c8d0fd5 ("serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume to system sleep callbacks")
Signed-off-by: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
[ryan: disable runtime PM before the status check, reword the code
 comment to what is established]
Signed-off-by: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
---
 drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
index a851b8f0e..7c809369e 100644
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -2107,23 +2107,56 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(struct device *dev)
 
 	/*
 	 * When no_console_suspend is set the console must remain active
-	 * across system sleep, so skip the force suspend path.
+	 * across system sleep, so skip the vote release.
 	 */
 	if (!console_suspend_enabled && uart_console(uport))
 		return 0;
 
-	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
+	/*
+	 * Disable runtime PM before checking the status, as
+	 * pm_runtime_force_suspend() did. Otherwise a dedicated wake IRQ could
+	 * runtime-resume a closed port after the check, before the wake IRQs
+	 * are armed, and the port would hold its votes for the whole sleep.
+	 */
+	pm_runtime_disable(dev);
+
+	/* A closed port is runtime suspended and has released its votes. */
+	if (pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev))
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * The serial core cannot runtime-suspend the port here (the PM core
+	 * holds a runtime PM reference on every device for the whole system
+	 * sleep transition), so the baud-rate OPP vote and the interconnect
+	 * bandwidth vote would stay in RPMh's sleep set and keep the CX rail
+	 * from collapsing. Release the votes, but keep the serial engine
+	 * clocked and its pins in the default state: on the Surface Pro 11,
+	 * power-collapsing the engine with pm_runtime_force_suspend() can
+	 * leave the link to the Surface Aggregator EC dead after resume.
+	 */
+	if (port->se.has_opp)
+		dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, 0);
+	ret = geni_icc_disable(&port->se);
+	if (ret)
+		pm_runtime_enable(dev);
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
-	int ret;
+	int ret = 0;
 	struct qcom_geni_serial_port *port = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
 
 	if (console_suspend_enabled || !uart_console(uport)) {
-		ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+		/* Restore the votes only if suspend released them. */
+		if (!pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev)) {
+			ret = geni_icc_enable(&port->se);
+			if (!ret && port->se.has_opp && port->clk_rate)
+				ret = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, port->clk_rate);
+		}
+		pm_runtime_enable(dev);
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
 	}
-- 
2.56.0

